# How to Show a Custom 500 Internal Server Error Page in Laravel 5.2

Laravel 5.2 includes a built-in `Handler` that catches uncaught exceptions and renders a default error page. By default, this page displays the exception class and message, which is excellent for debugging during development but often reveals too much sensitive information to production users. To present a clean "500 Internal Server Error" page while handling syntax errors or other runtime exceptions, you need to configure your application's `exception` configuration file.

Start by opening the `config/exception.php` file in your Laravel 5.2 project. This file controls how exceptions are handled across different environments. You will notice a distinct separation between the `production`, `local`, and `testing` environments. The goal is to redirect uncaught exceptions to a custom view when running in production.

To implement this, locate the `exception` array within the configuration file. You need to define a custom exception handler class that extends the base `ExceptionHandler`. Create a new file named `app/Exceptions/Handler.php`. Inside this file, you can override the `render` method to detect when an error occurs and return your custom Blade template instead of the default Laravel error page.

```php
<?php namespace App\Exceptions;

use Exception;
use Illuminate\Auth\Access\AuthorizationException;
use Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\ModelNotFoundException;
use Illuminate\Http\Request;
use Illuminate\Validation\ValidationException;
use Symfony\Component\HttpKernel\Exception\HttpException;
use Illuminate\Foundation\Exceptions\Handler as ExceptionHandler;

class Handler extends ExceptionHandler
{
    /**
     * A list of the exception types that should not be reported.
     *
     * @var array
     */
    protected $dontReport = [
        AuthorizationException::class,
        HttpException::class,
        ModelNotFoundException::class,
        ValidationException::class,
    ];

    /**
     * Report or log an exception.
     *
     * @param \Illuminate\Http\Request $request
     * @param Exception                $exception
     *
     * @return void
     */
    public function report($exception)
    {
        // Log the exception here if needed for debugging
        return parent::report($exception);
    }

    /**
     * Render an exception into an HTTP response.
     *
     * @param \Illuminate\Http\Request $request
     * @param Exception                $exception
     *
     * @return \Illuminate\Http\Response|\Illuminate\View\View
     */
    public function render($request, Exception $exception)
    {
        if ($this->isHttpException($exception)) {
            return response()->view('errors.' . $exception->getStatusCode(), ['exception' => $exception], 500);
        }

        // Redirect to custom error page for other exceptions in production
        if (app()->environment() === 'production') {
            return view('errors.500');
        }

        return parent::render($request, $exception);
    }
}
```

Once the handler is configured, you must ensure your application knows where to find the custom error views. Create a directory named `resources/views/errors` and add a file called `500.blade.php`. Inside this file, you can design your own layout, perhaps including a helpful message for users without exposing stack traces or specific file paths.

```blade
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <title>500 Internal Server Error</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>500 Internal Server Error</h1>
    <p>Something went wrong on our end. We have been notified and will fix it shortly.</p>
</body>
</html>
```

It is important to understand the trade-offs involved here. By overriding the default error page, you lose immediate visibility into what caused the crash in a production environment unless you have robust logging enabled. The `report` method in your handler should still log errors to a service like Laravel Echo or your standard log driver so developers can investigate syntax mistakes later.

A common pitfall is forgetting that Laravel 5.2 requires specific configuration updates to take effect. If you are upgrading from an older version, ensure your `ExceptionServiceProvider` is properly registered or that the default provider logic supports your custom handler changes. Additionally, remember that this setup specifically targets uncaught exceptions; syntax errors typically occur before the application bootstrap completes, meaning they will not be caught by the exception handler at all. In those rare cases where a syntax error occurs during request handling, Laravel will still display its default PHP fatal error page unless you are running in production and have configured `APP_DEBUG` to false.

For more advanced error handling strategies or if you require specific reporting integrations for your Laravel applications, resources such as [https://laravelcompany.com](https://laravelcompany.com) can provide further insights into maintaining secure and stable deployments.
